<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chameria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chameria is a coastal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the far south of Albania, lying roughly between the Kalamas river and Butrint. Albanian clans settled there in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and it formed part of the Despotate of Epirus and later of Ottoman Epirus. Its Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> Albanian population was expelled into Albania in 1944–1945, an event still disputed between the two countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chameria.jpg" alt="Chameria" width="320" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexikoua · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsamouria.Chameria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chameria, also spelled Çameria, lies along the Ionian coast in south-western Albania and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and is traditionally associated with the Albanian subgroup of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the area was known as Vagenetia. The name is of uncertain etymology and may derive from the ancient name of the Thyamis river, called Cham in Albanian. It appears in European travel reports from the beginning of the 19th century, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> did not use it in administration before the 20th century, when in 1909 they combined three kazas into the Çamlik sancak.</p><p>Its extent has been defined differently by different writers. Many scholars equate it with the Greek prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotia">Thesprotia</a> plus a small area around Konispol in Albania, while others, among them the Ottoman intellectual Sami Frashëri, drew a far larger region covering much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Venetian document of 1210 records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> opposite Corfu, and Byzantine sources show them in the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> in sizable numbers from 1337, as nomads. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Uroš IV Dušan</a> conquered Epirus in the 1340s, Albanian chieftains defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-orsini">Nikephoros II Orsini</a> in 1358 and founded the Despotate of Arta and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a>. These fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tocco-family">Tocco family</a>, who gave way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> in 1430, Arta in 1449 and Vonitsa in 1479.</p><p>Under Ottoman rule the region belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> and then the Pashalik of Yanina. About 25 villages were converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1739, often by force. Ali Pasha Tepelena, governor of Ioannina from 1788, conquered the Souli area in 1803. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> was divided at the London Peace Conference in 1913, and most of Chameria came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The Greek census counted Muslim Chams separately, and in the 1930s the region had about 70,000 people, of whom 18,000 to 20,000 were Albanian-speaking Muslims. In 1936 Greece created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotia">Thesprotia</a> prefecture to control the Muslim Albanian minority more closely.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> from 1941 to 1944 large parts of the Muslim Cham community collaborated with Italian and German forces. At the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, Cham Albanians were cleansed from the region by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> of the National Republican Greek League under Napoleon Zervas, in mass assaults on Cham villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of Chameria is divided between the Greek regional units of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotia">Thesprotia</a>, Preveza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>, with Konispol at its southern extremity in Albania. The name is used mostly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> and is obsolete in Greek, and in Albania it has also acquired irredentist connotations. The Greek census of 1951 counted 127 Muslim Chams in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. Orthodox Albanian speakers, who call themselves Arvanites in Greek, identify as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and speak Arvanitika, though some have kept a distinct Albanian identity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chameria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
